[1;33;40m  ---> Define a four-dimensional manifold and metric.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Load helper functions for component calculations.[0m


[1;33;40m   ### Scalar field and P(X) Lagrangian[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Define the scalar field on the manifold.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Define the gravitational coupling constant.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Define the arbitrary function P(X) which parameterises the scalar sector.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Define X as an abstract scalar for cleaner output of the final component equations.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> The kinetic scalar is X = -(1/2) g^{ab} nabla_a phi nabla_b phi.[0m
X = InputForm[-1/2*(CD[-a][Phi[]]*CD[a][Phi[]])]


[1;33;40m  ---> Write the covariant Lagrangian density (without the sqrt(-g) factor).[0m
L = InputForm[FuncP[-1/2*(CD[-a][Phi[]]*CD[a][Phi[]])] + RicciScalarCD[]/(2*GravitationalCoupling)]


[1;33;40m   ### Covariant field equations via VarD[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Vary the Lagrangian with respect to the inverse metric to obtain the Einstein equations.[0m
Metric EOM = InputForm[RicciCD[-a, -b]/(2*GravitationalCoupling) - (CD[-a][Phi[]]*CD[-b][Phi[]]*Derivative[1][FuncP][-1/2*Scalar[CD[-a][Phi[]]*CD[a][Phi[]]]])/2]


[1;33;40m  ---> Vary the Lagrangian with respect to the scalar field to obtain the Klein-Gordon equation.[0m
Scalar EOM = InputForm[CD[-a][CD[a][Phi[]]]*Derivative[1][FuncP][-1/2*Scalar[CD[-a][Phi[]]*CD[a][Phi[]]]] - CD[a][Phi[]]*CD[-b][CD[-a][Phi[]]]*CD[b][Phi[]]*Derivative[2][FuncP][-1/2*Scalar[CD[-a][Phi[]]*CD[a][Phi[]]]]]


[1;33;40m  ---> Replace the expanded kinetic scalar with the abstract X in all P(X) arguments for component work.[0m


[1;33;40m   ### Chart and metric components[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Set up spherical polar coordinates on the four-dimensional manifold.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Set up spherical polar coordinates.[0m


[3;30;46m            [displayed expression with 5 leaves]            (##)[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> Define the unknown scalar functions.[0m


[1;33;40m  ---> The line element takes the general static spherically symmetric form.[0m


